#usda 1.0
(
    doc = """Arena-facing wrapper over jescobars' `valve_rig.usdz`. Two jobs.

    1. RE-SCOPE THE TRAVEL.
    The source rig authors the hand-wheel's revolute travel as 539.7 deg -> 2879.8 deg,
    i.e. 6.5 full turns, faithful to a real multi-turn gate valve. Arena's `Openable`
    affordance normalises openness linearly over the joint's *limits*
    (`isaaclab_arena/utils/joint_utils.py:53-63`) and `OpenDoorTask` calls success at
    openness > 0.5, so against the raw limits a demonstration would have to complete 3.25
    revolutions inside one episode -- unreachable by teleoperation, and every demo would
    end by timeout and never be written to the HDF5.
    This layer overrides the limits so the modelled travel is exactly one turn, 0 -> 360
    deg. Openness then maps 0 -> 1 over that single turn and `OPENNESS_SUCCESS_THRESHOLD
    = 0.5` keeps its intended meaning of half a revolution -- the same span the procedural
    `valve_handwheel_v1.usda` modelled, so nothing downstream changes. To retune how much
    travel counts as fully open, edit `physics:upperLimit`.

    The range is rebased to zero rather than kept at the rig's authored 539.7 deg rest
    angle because Isaac Lab spawns an articulation with `init_state.joint_pos = {'.*':
    0.0}` and validates it against the limits; leaving the range at 539.7 -> 899.7 aborts
    startup with "The following joints have default positions out of the limits". Rebasing
    is sound here: a hand-wheel is a body of revolution about this very axis, so shifting
    its zero only changes the rotational phase the spokes rest at, not the geometry, the
    physics or the task.

    Scoping note for the thesis: the imitation-learning task is the manipulation skill
    (approach, grasp a spoke, rotate), not the full multi-turn actuation of a real gate
    valve. Bounding the modelled travel keeps openness a meaningful normalised measure of
    progress within an 8-20 s episode.

    2. KEEP THE MATERIALS ATTACHED.
    The rig's meshes bind to `/World/Looks/OmniPBR` and `/World/PhysicsMaterial`, which sit
    outside `</World/Valve>`. Referencing the valve subtree alone drops both bindings
    silently ("refers to a path outside the scope of the reference ... Ignoring"), and
    losing the physics material costs staticFriction 1.2 / dynamicFriction 1.0 -- the
    friction the G1's fingers need to hold a spoke without slipping. Both materials are
    therefore pulled in as children of the asset root and rebound here.

    3. UNBRAKE THE WHEEL.
    The source rig drives the hand-wheel with `damping = 100`, `stiffness = 0`,
    `targetVelocity = 0` and `maxForce = 1000`. A damping-only drive with zero target
    velocity is a viscous brake: the torque opposing rotation is `damping * angular_speed`,
    so turning the wheel at even 10 deg/s demands ~1000 N*m -- exactly the drive's force
    ceiling. The G1 grips a spoke at a radius of 0.10 m and its arm actuators cap out around
    25 N*m at the shoulder, so the hand delivers a couple of N*m at best. Measured in the
    headset on 2026-08-18: the wheel does not move at all under teleoperation.

Damping is lowered to 0.01. USD authors angular drive gains per degree while Isaac Lab
    reports them per radian, so this reads back as `joint_damping = 0.573` in sim (the
    original 100 read back as 5730). ~1 N*m then turns the wheel at ~100 deg/s: half a turn,
    which is the success threshold, takes a couple of seconds. `stiffness` stays 0 (no spring
    back to a rest angle) and `physxJoint:jointFriction = 0.1` is left alone -- a small static
    threshold that gives the wheel a "sticky" break-away without blocking it.

    This value was NOT verified synthetically. `test_valve_torque.py` applies a known torque
    to the wheel body with `set_external_force_and_torque` and reads back exactly 0.00 deg
    even at 50 N*m, i.e. the external-wrench path is a no-op on this backend (same family as
    the `PhysxManager has no attribute '_get_backend_utils'` warning at startup). The only
    valid test is teleoperation. The value therefore errs on the easy side: raise it toward
    0.05-0.1 once the robot can turn the wheel, if a heavier valve is wanted for realism.

    Deliberately NOT inherited from the source stage: `/Environment/defaultLight` (would
    fight Arena's dome light), `/Render/*` and `/Viewport_Measure` (authoring leftovers).

    Structure mirrors `valve_handwheel_v1.usda`: defaultPrim is the articulation root
    itself, which is what Arena's asset registry spawns. Geometry and physics are
    referenced, never copied.
    """
    defaultPrim = "Valve"
    kilogramsPerUnit = 1
    metersPerUnit = 1
    upAxis = "Z"
)

def Xform "Valve" (
    prepend references = @./valve_rig.usdz@</World/Valve>
)
{
    def Scope "Looks"
    {
        def Material "OmniPBR" (
            prepend references = @./valve_rig.usdz@</World/Looks/OmniPBR>
        )
        {
        }
    }

    def Material "PhysicsMaterial" (
        prepend references = @./valve_rig.usdz@</World/PhysicsMaterial>
    )
    {
    }

    over "RevoluteJoint"
    {
        float physics:lowerLimit = 0
        float physics:upperLimit = 360

        # 3. LET THE ROBOT ACTUALLY TURN IT. See the third block of the doc above.
        float drive:angular:physics:damping = 0.01
    }

    over "handwheel"
    {
        over "node_50_AL_250_B7_8_A"
        {
            over "mesh_50_AL_250_B7_8_A_stl"
            {
                rel material:binding = </Valve/Looks/OmniPBR>
                rel material:binding:physics = </Valve/PhysicsMaterial>
            }
        }
    }

    over "valve_body"
    {
        over "valve_model"
        {
            over "valve_model_stl_001"
            {
                rel material:binding = </Valve/Looks/OmniPBR>
            }
        }
    }
}
